      "claim": "Rigid primary-amine-containing molecules show high intestinal absorption: AMG 221 was completely absorbed from the gut lumen in rats (C154), and the M19-associated record (A-79035) showed an intestinal fraction reaching portal blood of 0.88 (C151).",
      "card_ids": ["C151","C154"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Rigid basic alkaloids show high Caco-2 permeability: strychnine Papp 3.11×10^-5 cm/s (C149) and huperzine A Papp 16–31 ×10^-6 cm/s at pH 6.8–7.4 (C160, C164), supporting absorption of rigid amine-containing scaffolds.",
      "card_ids": ["C149","C160","C164"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "An adamantane-scaffold sulfonamide had aqueous solubility >100 µg/mL (C131, C144), and the query's ionizable primary amine is not solubility-limited based on supplied records.",
      "card_ids": ["C131","C144"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No L4 record provides affirmative evidence of F<20% for a small rigid primary amine; low-solubility/low-permeability records involve non-transferable lipophilic or large molecules (HBCDD C133/C168, cyclandelate C130, CBD C166, BMS-182751 C134).",
      "card_ids": ["C130","C133","C134","C166","C168"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Combined with L1 human gold records for small polar/basic molecules meeting or exceeding F=20% (gabapentin ~60% C06, entecavir 100% C07, minocycline 90–100% C01/C15/C25, nemonoxacin ~95–100% C08/C17/C28, cocaine 20–60% C02/C11/C14/C21/C26, theophylline 53% fasted C12, MK-8591 high C27) and L3 oral-exposure records for adamantane-containing/primary-amine molecules (saxagliptin C71/C77/C82, M05 C107/C109, gabapentin C79, tranexamic acid C108), the prediction remains high.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C02","C06","C07","C08","C11","C12","C14","C15","C17","C21","C25","C26","C27","C28","C71","C77","C79","C82","C107","C108","C109"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "high",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C128","C131","C132","C142","C144","C149","C150","C151","C154","C156","C160","C164"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct amantadine-specific absolute oral bioavailability record is present in the supplied cards; support is transfer-based.",
    "L4 absorption/permeability records do not quantify systemic F or first-pass metabolism; no matched IV comparator is available for the query.",
    "The query's full ionization at physiological pH and low logD leave passive permeability uncertainty, though small size and paracellular absorption may compensate.",
    "No L5 gut-wall efflux/intestinal-metabolism evidence is yet available.",
    "No direct human F record for the closest adamantane-containing structural analogs is supplied."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high and is strengthened by L4. Direct intestinal absorption evidence for small polar/rigid primary-amine-containing molecules (gabapentin human Fa 74% and rat Fa 80–98%; AMG 221 complete gut absorption; A-79035 portal F0 0.88; high Caco-2 permeability for rigid basic alkaloids; adamantane sulfonamide solubility >100 µg/mL) supports the absorption component of F>=20%. No affirmative sub-20% evidence applies to the query. Confidence is raised to high, with the absence of a direct amantadine F record noted as a gap."
}

